description:
## Bug

`encode()` and `encode_part()` document `line_length` as "maximum number of encoded bytes per line (1–255)", but with `line_length=1` every byte that requires a 2-byte escape sequence produces a line of length 2 (violating the maximum) plus a spurious empty `\r\n` line before it.

## Root cause

In `encode_body` (encode.rs line 135), when `col + 2 > line_length` the encoder flushes the current partial line before emitting an escape pair. When `col == 0` and `line_length == 1`, this emits an empty `\r\n` even though there is nothing to flush, then emits the 2-byte pair, then immediately flushes again at the `col >= line_length` check — producing:

```
\r\n       (spurious empty line)
=<escaped>\r\n  (2-char line, exceeds line_length=1)
```

## Impact

Low severity: `line_length=1` is a pathological value that no real yEnc encoder would use. However, the stated API contract is violated. The Rust decoder handles empty lines and 2-char lines without error, so round-trips still succeed. External decoders may not.

## Fix option 1 — Document the constraint

Change the doc comment to "minimum effective value is 2; escape pairs always occupy 2 columns" and clamp to `line_length.max(2)` internally.

## Fix option 2 — Encode-time assert

`assert!(line_length >= 2, "line_length must be >= 2 to accommodate escape sequences")`

## Test to add

```rust
#[test]
fn no_line_exceeds_line_length_with_escapes() {
    // line_length=2: escape pairs fit exactly; no line should exceed 2.
    let payload = vec![19u8; 10]; // all bytes encode to '=' (need escaping)
    let encoded = encode(&payload, "t.bin", 2);
    for line in encoded.split(|&b| b == b'\n') {
        let line = line.strip_suffix(b"\r").unwrap_or(line);
        if line.starts_with(b"=ybegin") || line.starts_with(b"=yend") || line.is_empty() { continue; }
        assert!(line.len() <= 2, "line too long: {:?}", line);
    }
}
```
